codex
The new gate can report success while literal SQL violations are present in additional crate modules, so it does not reliably enforce the conformance checks it adds. The wiring and tests pass, but these false negatives undermine the publish-precondition gate.

Full review comments:

- [P2] Scan schema modules for forbidden backfills — /home/coreyt/projects/fathomdb-worktrees/orch-0.8.20-s30/scripts/check-c1-conformance.sh:447-448
  When a migration/backfill is added in another schema source file (for example a new module under `src/rust/crates/fathomdb-schema/src` containing `INSERT INTO canonical_attributes ...`), these `absent` probes still inspect only `lib.rs`, so the gate exits 0 even though C1-Q2 is violated. Since the checker explicitly claims to catch literal pinned-table spellings, the negative scan should cover the schema crate's migration source, not just the current top-level file.

- [P2] Scan engine modules for forbidden FTS DDL — /home/coreyt/projects/fathomdb-worktrees/orch-0.8.20-s30/scripts/check-c1-conformance.sh:579-581
  If engine-side creation of `property_search_index` is introduced in a new engine module, this probe still reads only `src/lib.rs`, so `CREATE VIRTUAL TABLE main.property_search_index ...` in another `fathomdb-engine/src/*.rs` file passes the conformance gate. The contract is about the engine not owning this DDL, so this negative assertion needs to cover the engine source tree or every module that can contain engine SQL.
